<attribute name="name" nodeId="7067307e">Determine required testing tools</attribute>
<attribute name="guid" nodeId="892045fd">_Zf3aQB2XEeSDYtcxfT4ZFw</attribute>
<attribute name="presentationName" nodeId="572246d">Determine required testing tools</attribute>
<attribute name="briefDescription" nodeId="757af592"></attribute>
<attribute name="suppressed" nodeId="50e7940f">false</attribute>
<attribute name="orderingGuide" nodeId="5903b04b"></attribute>
<attribute name="variabilityType" nodeId="6f8c9fc5">na</attribute>
<attribute name="sectionName" nodeId="f75ea0e4"></attribute>
<attribute name="sectionDescription" nodeId="229a13e">&lt;p&gt;
    Using a testing tool to support activities in the test effort is a common practice. Testing tools typically have
    features that support and automate a number of activities in the test lifecycle, including creation of test cases, test
    execution automation, code monitoring, data generation, defect management, test result analysis, coverage monitoring
    and status reporting. Test management tools are influential in reducing costs, increasing efficiencies, streamlining
    processes and coordinating tasks among test resources. Making a decision to use and to select a test management tool
    should be discussed with stakeholders.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;</attribute>
